CAT = 'rbtDoc'; SEG = 'SDOC'
Q = [
("At pickup, a client's mother says the bedtime routine the team taught has stopped working and she wants to try a reward chart instead. The supervisor visits on Friday. What should the RBT do?",
 'Tell the supervisor about it today', ['Help her set up the reward chart now', "Hold it for the supervisor's Friday visit", 'Tell her to keep using the plan as written'],
 'Caregiver concerns go to the supervisor in a timely manner, so the RBT relays it now rather than waiting for the visit. Helping design a new chart changes the plan without the supervisor.'),
('A client hits a peer at recess. His behavior plan defines hitting, says to block, redirect him to the swings and record it, and the RBT has been trained on it. The supervisor is off site. What should the RBT do?',
 'Block, redirect and record it', ['Call the supervisor before acting', 'End recess and wait for the supervisor', 'Try a short time-out instead'],
 'When the written plan covers the behavior and the RBT is trained on it, she carries the plan out and records it. Escalation is for what the plan does not cover; calling first delays a procedure that is already decided.'),
("A client's plan says to record every instance of ear covering with the time and setting. During a fire drill he covers his ears twice. The RBT should:",
 'Record both with time and setting', ['Text the supervisor instead of recording', 'Record it only if it lasts a while', 'Tell the supervisor at the next visit'],
 'The plan already says what to do: record each instance with its time and setting. A message to the supervisor does not replace the data the plan asks for, and waiting for a visit loses the detail.'),
("A father asks the RBT what the token board on the table is for. The supervisor has already explained the plan to him and asked the RBT to answer simple questions about how it runs. The RBT's best step is to:",
 'Explain how the token board works', ['Tell him to ask the supervisor', 'Say the plan is confidential', 'Promise to change it if he wants'],
 "Describing how an approved procedure runs, when the supervisor has asked her to, is within the RBT's role. Sending him away for a simple answer is unhelpful; promising changes is a plan decision she cannot make."),
('A parent texts the RBT on Saturday that the client bit a sibling hard enough to break skin. The next session is Monday. What should the RBT do?',
 'Report it to the supervisor now', ['Tell the supervisor on Monday', 'Tell the parent to add a time-out', "Log it on Monday's data sheet only"],
 'A serious new safety concern goes to the supervisor right away, following workplace policy. Telling her on Monday is the right person at the wrong time.'),
('A father asks the RBT to stop the token board because he thinks it bribes his son. The plan was written with his consent last month. The RBT should:',
 'Share his concern with the supervisor', ['Stop the token board as he requested', 'Keep it and explain it is not bribery', 'Fade the tokens out over a few sessions'],
 'Caregiver concerns about a procedure go to the supervisor, who can discuss it with the father. Stopping, defending or fading the board are all plan decisions.'),
('Mid-session the RBT finds the reinforcer named in the plan, a tablet, has a dead battery. The plan lists a backup reinforcer, stickers, for this case. The RBT should:',
 'Switch to the listed backup', ['Call the supervisor for a new one', 'Stop the session until it charges', 'Run trials with no reinforcer'],
 'The plan already names a backup for this case, so the RBT uses it and notes the swap. Calling for direction the plan already gives, stopping the session or dropping reinforcement are all worse.'),
('Halfway through a session, the RBT sees that the new data sheet has no place to record prompt level, which the plan requires. The best step is to:',
 'Ask the supervisor how to record it', ['Stop until the supervisor replies', 'Use her own codes and keep them private', 'Add the prompt levels later from memory'],
 'A data recording problem is a reason to seek direction promptly while keeping the session going. Stopping the session waiting for a reply loses teaching time; inventing codes or recording from memory makes the data unreliable.'),
('An RBT has three questions for her supervisor: a new self-injury pattern, a missing data sheet and an upcoming schedule change. Which should she raise first?',
 'The new self-injury pattern', ['The missing data sheet', 'The upcoming schedule change', 'Whichever came up first'],
 'Client safety comes first when prioritizing what to bring to the supervisor. Data and scheduling matters follow.'),
("An RBT's supervisor is unreachable during a session in which the client's aggression is far above anything in the plan. What is the best next step?",
 'Follow the chain of command', ["Wait for the supervisor's reply", 'Try a new procedure on her own', 'Ask the parent what to do'],
 'When the supervisor cannot be reached, the RBT follows the workplace chain of command to get clinical direction. Waiting or improvising leaves a safety issue unaddressed.'),
("For two weeks an RBT's mastered-target probes show 100% while the same skills fall apart with other staff. What should she do?",
 'Report the data irregularity', ['Keep probing as written', 'Lower her scoring standard', 'Report it after another month'],
 'Data that do not match across staff is an irregularity the RBT brings to the supervisor now. Waiting another month keeps a possibly wrong picture of mastery in the record.'),
('An RBT is told to start a new extinction procedure tomorrow but has only read the plan and never practiced it. The best step is to:',
 'Ask the supervisor for training', ['Start it and ask questions afterward', 'Ask the supervisor to excuse her from it', 'Have a coworker show her before session'],
 "Training needs are taken to the supervisor before running a procedure. Asking to be excused does not get the client the procedure; a coworker's demonstration is not training verified by the supervisor."),
("A lead RBT on site tells a new RBT to change a client's reinforcer, but the supervisor's plan names a different one. The new RBT should:",
 'Check with the supervisor first', ["Follow the lead RBT's change", 'Use both reinforcers to be safe', 'Follow it until the supervisor calls'],
 'Only the supervisor can change the plan. The RBT checks before changing anything; following the change until the supervisor calls is still running an unapproved change.'),
("An RBT notices that her interval data and her coworker's for the same session agree only about half the time. Their supervisor's next visit is in two weeks. The best step is to:",
 'Contact the supervisor about it now', ['Raise it with the supervisor in two weeks', 'Keep whichever data set looks steadier', 'Rewrite the definition together'],
 "Low agreement means the data cannot be trusted, so it goes to the supervisor now rather than at the next visit. Picking one data set or rewriting the definition are not the RBT's calls."),
("A client's mother mentions that his ADHD medication dose was doubled two days ago and asks the RBT to keep it off the record until they see how it goes. The RBT should:",
 'Explain it must go to the supervisor', ['Keep it private as she asked', 'Tell the supervisor only if behavior changes', 'Tell her to ask the doctor first'],
 'A medication change can affect behavior and the data, so the RBT explains it must be shared with the supervisor now. Waiting for behavior to change means the data cannot be read correctly.'),
("A client's parents tell the RBT they have separated and the client now splits his week between two homes. This information should be:",
 'Reported to the supervisor promptly', ['Kept private as a family matter', 'Reported to the supervisor next month', "Shared with the client's teacher too"],
 "A change in living situation can affect behavior, so it goes to the supervisor promptly. Next month is too late, and the teacher is not told without the family's consent."),
('A client has missed five of the last eight sessions because of transportation problems. What should the RBT do?',
 'Document it and tell the supervisor', ['Add extra trials to catch him up', 'Remind the family that attendance matters', 'Wait for the supervisor to notice it'],
 'A pattern of missed sessions is documented and raised with the supervisor. Waiting for the supervisor to spot it in the record leaves it unaddressed; extra trials change the plan.'),
('A client arrives yawning and says he was awake most of the night because of a storm. Why should the RBT note this in her session report?',
 'Lack of sleep may affect responding', ['Sleep loss changes the session length', 'It may mean the home sleep plan failed', 'The parents must sign any health note'],
 'Sleep loss is a variable that can change how a client responds that day. Reporting it helps the supervisor read the session data correctly.'),
("A client's school switched him to a new classroom with a different teacher this week. What is the best reason to report it to the supervisor?",
 'It might affect his progress', ['It ends the school program', 'It changes his billing code', 'It requires a new consent form'],
 'A change in staff and setting is a variable that might affect client progress, so it is reported in a timely manner.'),
('During a session a client keeps rubbing his ear and crying, which he has never done before. Besides following illness policy, the RBT should:',
 'Report it to the supervisor that day', ['Put the crying on extinction for now', "Tell the supervisor at next week's visit", 'Tell the parent it is attention-seeking'],
 "A new sign that may be pain or illness is reported the same day, besides following illness policy. Next week's visit is too late, and putting possible pain on extinction is unsafe."),
("A family says a new baby will be born next month and the client will stay with relatives for two weeks. The RBT's best step is to:",
 'Tell the supervisor about the change', ['Plan extra sessions for after the trip', 'Start a new sibling program early', 'Tell the supervisor after the trip'],
 'A schedule and family change is passed to the supervisor now, so the plan can be adjusted ahead of time. Telling her afterwards misses the chance to plan; adding sessions or programs is her call.'),
('An RBT is writing up a session in which a client screamed after a peer took his train. Which sentence belongs in her note?',
 'Screamed 2 min after peer took train', ['Got jealous and screamed at peer', 'Screamed to get the train back', 'Had a meltdown over sharing'],
 'An objective note describes what was seen and timed. Jealousy, a meltdown or wanting the train back are interpretations of feelings or function, not observations.'),
('A parent asks the RBT why her son had a hard session. Which reply stays objective and within her role?',
 'He cried for 6 minutes at cleanup', ['He was testing his limits today', 'He is frustrated by the new program', 'He has seemed sad since the family move'],
 'Describing what happened, in observable terms, is objective. Guesses about testing limits or feelings are interpretations and clinical judgments.'),
('An RBT forgot to start the timer for one 10-minute interval block. She is fairly sure the client was on task the whole time. What should her note show for that block?',
 'That the block was not recorded', ['On task for the whole block', "Yesterday's data for that block", 'An average of the other blocks'],
 'Data come from observation recorded at the time; a confident memory is still an estimate. The note states the gap plainly so the supervisor does not read an estimate as data.'),
("A session ended 20 minutes early because the parent picked the client up. The parent asks the RBT to write the full scheduled time so the family's hours are not cut. The RBT should:",
 'Record the actual times', ['Write the scheduled times once', 'Split the difference in the note', 'Leave the times off the note'],
 "Documentation must match what actually occurred, as legal, funder and workplace rules require, whoever asks. She can pass the parent's concern about hours to the supervisor."),
('A client fell from a chair and bumped her head during a session. Which is the best entry for the incident report?',
 'Fell at 2:15, red mark on forehead', ['Clumsy fall, but she seemed fine after', 'Minor accident with no lasting harm', 'Fell because she was not being careful'],
 'An incident report states what happened, when and what was observed. Words like clumsy or not careful are judgments, not observations.'),
("After a session, the RBT's supervisor asks by phone how the new matching program went with the client. Which summary is most objective?",
 '7 of 10 correct, 2 of them prompted', ['She is really getting the hang of it', 'It went really well overall today', 'She did better than she did last week'],
 'Reporting trial counts and prompts gives the supervisor objective information. General impressions do not show what actually happened.'),
("An RBT realizes after submitting a session note that she entered the wrong client's data. What should she do?",
 'Correct it per policy, tell supervisor', ['Fix it quietly in the next session note', 'Tell the supervisor, leave it as is', 'Delete the note and write a new one'],
 "An error in a submitted note is corrected through the workplace's correction process and the supervisor is told. Another client's data in this record is also a confidentiality problem, so telling her but leaving it as is is not enough; deleting a note breaks the record."),
]
